---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ipsas/ipsas24.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Objective, Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Approved Budgets — Original & Final" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Presentation of Budget vs Actual Amounts" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Presentation Format & Level of Aggregation" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Comparable Basis" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Multi-Year Budgets" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Note Disclosures — Basis, Period & Scope" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Reconciliation to the Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Effective Date & Amendment Timeline" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Relationship to GFS & Related Frameworks" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement / Process Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="IPSAS 24 Presentation of Budget Information in Financial Statements is the current, active standard issued by the IPSASB for presenting approved budget and actual amounts, reconciliations, and comparable-basis disclosures alongside the financial statements. Effective 1 January 2009."
    historical="IPSAS 24 complements IPSAS 1 and GFS 1 — it mandates that budget information be presented in the notes or a combined statement, reconciled to the financial statements on a comparable basis."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

  <div class="wrap">
  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — OBJECTIVE, SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Objective, Scope &amp; Definitions (paras. 1–7)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Objective &amp; scope</div>
        <div class="principle">Requires a comparison of budget amounts and actual amounts (from budget execution) in the financial statements of entities REQUIRED, or ELECTING, to make their approved budget(s) publicly available — plus disclosure of material differences between them, discharging accountability and demonstrating compliance.</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Applies to entities preparing accrual-basis financial statements that ARE required, or ELECT, to make approved budgets publicly available</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Does NOT require budgets to be made public, and doesn't require comparisons against budgets that AREN'T made public (para. 5). Separate approved budgets for individual agencies/programs within a consolidated entity are common — this Standard applies wherever a budget for the entity, or a component, is publicly available (para. 6).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions (para. 7)</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Approved budget</div><div class="glossary-def">The expenditure authority derived from laws, appropriation bills, ordinances, and other decisions related to anticipated revenue/receipts for the budget period.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Original budget</div><div class="glossary-def">The initial approved budget for the budget period.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Final budget</div><div class="glossary-def">The original budget adjusted for reserves, carry-overs, transfers, allocations, supplemental appropriations, and other authorized changes.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Comparable basis</div><div class="glossary-def">Actual amounts on the SAME accounting basis, classification basis, entities, and period as the approved budget.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — APPROVED BUDGETS: ORIGINAL & FINAL ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Approved Budgets — Original &amp; Final (paras. 8–13)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">What makes a budget "approved"</div>
        <div class="principle">Reflects anticipated revenues/receipts and legislatively-approved expenses/expenditures — NOT a forward estimate or projection based on assumptions that may never occur. The critical feature: authority to withdraw funds from the treasury for agreed purposes is provided by a legislative body or other appropriate authority.</div>
        <div class="note">If a budget isn't approved before the period begins, the original budget is whatever was FIRST approved for that budget year (para. 10). Some jurisdictions hold the entity accountable to the ORIGINAL budget; others to the FINAL budget (para. 9).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What changes the original into the final</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Automatically ROLLED-FORWARD appropriations covering prior-year commitments/obligations/encumbrances (e.g. outstanding purchase orders)</span>
          <span>SUPPLEMENTAL appropriations — e.g. for war, natural disasters, or a revenue shortfall</span>
          <span>Internal TRANSFERS between budget heads/line items for changing funding priorities</span>
          <span>Cutbacks to maintain overall fiscal discipline</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">"Actual amount" describes the amounts resulting from budget EXECUTION — sometimes called "budget out-turn" or "budget execution" in different jurisdictions (para. 13).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — PRESENTATION OF BUDGET VS ACTUAL AMOUNTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Presentation of a Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts (paras. 14–20)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Three required elements, per level of legislative oversight (para. 14)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The ORIGINAL and FINAL budget amounts</span>
          <span>The ACTUAL amounts, on a COMPARABLE basis</span>
          <span>By way of NOTE, an explanation of MATERIAL differences — unless included in other public documents issued alongside the financial statements, with a cross-reference</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Presentation options: a SEPARATE Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts, OR additional BUDGET COLUMNS in the existing financial statements — but the latter is only permitted when statements and budget are on a comparable basis (para. 22, see §5). Where BOTH original and final budgets must be publicly available, jurisdictional guidance usually determines which one the material-difference explanation is measured against; absent guidance, entities may choose actual-vs-original (performance focus) or actual-vs-final (compliance focus) (para. 17).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — PRESENTATION FORMAT & LEVEL OF AGGREGATION ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Presentation Format &amp; Level of Aggregation (paras. 21–27)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Which financial statements carry the budget columns?</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Budget covers the FULL set of financial statements (accrual basis) → additional budget columns can be added to ALL of them</span>
          <span>Budget presented only as CERTAIN statements (e.g. a budget statement of financial performance, or a cash-flow-only budget) → additional columns go only in THOSE statements the budget itself adopts (para. 24)</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Level of aggregation — matching legislative oversight</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Budget documents often provide granular detail, aggregated into broad classes under common "budget heads" for legislative approval. Disclosure at that SAME broad-class level ensures comparisons match the level of oversight actually exercised by the legislature or authority — aggregation to avoid information overload involves professional JUDGMENT (paras. 25, 27).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — COMPARABLE BASIS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Comparable Basis (paras. 31–36)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The non-negotiable rule</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">ALL budget-vs-actual comparisons SHALL be presented on a basis COMPARABLE to the budget — same accounting basis, classification, entities, and period</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Government adopts ACCRUAL for financial statements, CASH for its budget → real basis differences are common; recompiling separate agency budgets for consolidated presentation does NOT involve revising the approved budget itself (para. 33)</span>
          <span>Statistical-reporting-model budgets may cover only the GENERAL GOVERNMENT SECTOR — narrower than the full consolidated entity (para. 35, cross-ref IPSAS 22)</span>
          <span>Different levels of government may or may not be CONSOLIDATED, depending on control relationships — this Standard's requirements only bite where an approved budget for the CONTROLLED entities/activities is publicly available (para. 36)</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — MULTI-YEAR BUDGETS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Multi-Year Budgets (paras. 37–38)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">A series of annual targets, not one lump figure</div>
        <div class="principle">Some governments approve MULTI-YEAR budgets rather than separate annual ones. Conventionally, these comprise a series of annual budgets or targets — the approved budget for each component annual period reflects that period's application of the multi-year budgetary policy.</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>A biennial budget with two clearly separated annual approved amounts → original/final identifiable for EACH year normally</span>
          <span>Unused first-year appropriations legally rolled into year two → year two's ORIGINAL budget increases for the carry-over</span>
          <span>A multi-period budget with NO annual separation → judgment required to attribute amounts to each annual period (e.g. capital acquisitions occurring in year one, plus that year's share of recurring items)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Entities with multi-period budgets are ENCOURAGED to provide additional note disclosure about the relationship between budget and actual amounts across the whole budget period.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — NOTE DISCLOSURES: BASIS, PERIOD & SCOPE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Note Disclosures of Budgetary Basis, Period &amp; Scope (paras. 39–46)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Three mandatory explanatory notes</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The BUDGETARY BASIS and CLASSIFICATION BASIS adopted in the approved budget (para. 39)</span>
          <span>The PERIOD of the approved budget, where it differs from the financial statements' reporting period (para. 43)</span>
          <span>The ENTITIES included in the approved budget (para. 45)</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Why these matter — classification schemes can diverge widely</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Budgets classified by ECONOMIC NATURE (compensation, goods/services) or by FUNCTION (health, education) — potentially matching or differing from the financial statements' own classification</span>
          <span>Budgets classified by SPECIFIC PROGRAM (e.g. poverty reduction) or performance outcomes (e.g. graduation rates) — often entirely different from financial-statement classification</span>
          <span>Separate RECURRENT (operating) vs CAPITAL budgets are common (para. 41)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Disclosing the entities encompassed by the budget lets users see how far the entity's activities are actually SUBJECT to an approved budget, and how the "budget entity" differs from the full reporting entity in the financial statements (para. 46).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — RECONCILIATION TO THE FINANCIAL STATEMENTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Reconciliation of Actual Amounts to the Financial Statements (paras. 47–53)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required WHEN budget and statements aren't on a comparable basis</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Accrual-basis budget → reconcile to total revenues, total expenses, and net cash flows (operating/investing/financing)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Non-accrual-basis budget → reconcile to net cash flows only (operating/investing/financing)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Presented on the face of the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts, or in the notes — identifying BASIS, TIMING, and ENTITY differences separately.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Three types of difference (para. 48)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Difference type</span><span>Cause</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Basis</span><span>Budget on a different accounting basis than the financial statements (e.g. cash budget, accrual statements)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Timing</span><span>Budget period differs from the financial statements' reporting period</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Entity</span><span>Budget omits programs/entities that ARE part of the reporting entity</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">When budget and financial statements share the same basis, entities, period, AND presentation format, NO reconciliation is required — only identifying the differences (para. 50). Comparative (prior-period) information is NOT required for the budget-actual comparison itself (para. 52).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — EFFECTIVE DATE & AMENDMENT TIMELINE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Effective Date &amp; Amendment Timeline (paras. 54–55)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Amendment history at a glance</div>
        <div class="principle">Base Standard: periods beginning on/after <strong>1 January 2009</strong>.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Effective</span><span>Change</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2017</span><span>Para. 55 (IPSAS 33); para. 26 (Improvements to IPSAS 2015)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2018</span><span>Para. 4 deleted; paras. 3, 46 amended — The Applicability of IPSAS (Apr 2016)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2019</span><span>Paras. 21, 22, 24 — Improvements to IPSAS, 2018</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>—</span><span>Para. 48 — IPSAS 42, Social Benefits (applied alongside)</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — RELATIONSHIP TO GFS & RELATED FRAMEWORKS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Relationship to GFS &amp; Related Statistical Frameworks — No IAS/IFRS Equivalent</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A budget-accountability Standard with no private-sector counterpart</div>
        <div class="principle">Like IPSAS 22 and IPSAS 23, IPSAS 24 has no IFRS equivalent — the budgetary accountability cycle it addresses is a distinctly public sector concept. Its "Basis for Conclusions" notes the IPSASB considered the IASB's Convergence Program, but budget-actual comparisons for LEGISLATIVELY APPROVED spending authority simply don't have a private-sector parallel.</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Government Finance Statistics (GFS) frameworks (IMF GFSM, and the 2008 SNA underlying IPSAS 22) often use DIFFERENT classification bases than IPSAS financial statements — e.g. GFS's broader treatment of social benefits vs. IPSAS 42's narrower cash-transfer-only definition (§8)</span>
          <span>Where a jurisdiction's budget follows a GFS-consistent classification, the reconciliation required by §8 becomes the practical bridge between GFS-basis budget reporting and IPSAS-basis financial statements</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — STATEMENT / PROCESS MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement / Process Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Budget disclosure decision map</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>1. Is the entity REQUIRED, or does it ELECT, to make an approved budget publicly available? If no → this Standard doesn't apply (§1)</span>
          <span>2. Are the financial statements and the budget on a COMPARABLE basis (same accounting basis, classification, entities, period)? (§5)</span>
          <span>3a. YES → present original/final budget and actual amounts as ADDITIONAL COLUMNS in the existing IPSAS statements (§3)</span>
          <span>3b. NO → present a SEPARATE Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts, and RECONCILE actual amounts to the financial statements, identifying basis/timing/entity differences (§8)</span>
          <span>4. Disclose the budgetary basis, period, and scope of entities covered by the budget (§7)</span>
          <span>5. Explain MATERIAL differences between budget and actual amounts, in the notes or by cross-reference to other public documents (§3)</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Concept-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>IPSAS 24 concept</span><span>Statement(s)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Original &amp; final budget amounts</span><span><span class="badge bc">Budget</span></span><span>Additional columns, or a separate statement</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Actual amounts, comparable basis</span><span><span class="badge bc">Budget</span></span><span>Same basis/classification/entities/period as the budget</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Material budget-vs-actual differences</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Or cross-referenced to other public documents</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Reconciliation to the financial statements</span><span><span class="badge bc">Budget</span> <span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Required only where basis differs</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Budgetary basis, period, scope</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory explanatory disclosures</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IPSAS 24 connects across the IPSAS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 1</span><span>The budget comparison is a component of a complete set of financial statements where the budget is publicly available (§1)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 2</span><span>Cash flow classification structure underpins the reconciliation for non-accrual-basis budgets (§8)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 3</span><span>Qualitative characteristics basis for accounting policy development referenced when determining aggregation levels (§4)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 22</span><span>Where a budget is prepared for the GENERAL GOVERNMENT SECTOR specifically, GGS disclosures under IPSAS 22 directly support this Standard's comparisons (§5, §10)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 42</span><span>Social benefits classification differences between GFS-basis budgets and IPSAS financial statements — a common reconciling item (§8)</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Appropriation</div><div class="glossary-def">Authorization granted by a legislative body to allocate funds for purposes specified by the legislature or similar authority.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Budgetary basis</div><div class="glossary-def">The accrual, cash, or other accounting basis adopted in the legislatively approved budget.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Annual budget</div><div class="glossary-def">An approved budget for one year — excludes forward estimates or projections beyond the budget period.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Multi-year budget</div><div class="glossary-def">An approved budget for more than one year, again excluding forward estimates or projections.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Original budget</div><div class="glossary-def">The initial approved budget for the budget period.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Final budget</div><div class="glossary-def">The original budget adjusted for all authorized reserves, transfers, and legislative changes applicable to the period.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bc">Budget</span> Statement of comparison of budget and actual amounts
        <span class="badge bp">Notes</span> Notes to the financial statements
      </div>
    </div>


  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
